<header><p class="st-kicker">sultanate · Maritime Southeast Asia</p><h1 class="st-title">Yogyakarta Sultanate</h1><p class="st-meta">1755 CE – present · capital Yogyakarta</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Javanese sultanate created by the division of Mataram in 1755, which backed Indonesian independence and still rules its city as a special region.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-giyanti">Treaty of Giyanti</a> of 1755, brokered by the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/dutch-east-india-company-territories">Dutch East India Company</a>, ended a long succession war by splitting the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/mataram-sultanate">Mataram Sultanate</a> in two. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hamengkubuwono-i">Prince Mangkubumi</a> received the western half and became <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sultan">Sultan</a> Hamengkubuwono I, building a new kraton at Yogyakarta, while the eastern half became the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/surakarta-sunanate">Surakarta Sunanate</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The sultan ruled as a Muslim king and heir to Javanese kingship, with an elaborate court culture of gamelan, wayang, dance and batik. Royal lands supported nobles through apanages. British troops sacked the kraton in 1812, and the Yogyakarta prince <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diponegoro">Diponegoro</a> led the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/java-war">Java War</a> against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> from 1825 to 1830. <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/pakualaman">Pakualaman</a> was carved from it in 1812.</p><h2>To the present</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sultan">Sultan</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hamengkubuwono-ix">Hamengkubuwono IX</a> sided with the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/indonesia">Republic of Indonesia</a>, which made Yogyakarta its capital from 1946 to 1949. It became a Special Region in 1950, and a 2012 law confirmed that the reigning sultan serves as its governor. Hamengkubuwono X reigns today.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/yogyakarta-sultanate.jpg" alt="Yogyakarta Sultanate" width="320" height="214"><figcaption>Image: RaFaDa20631 · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Wirabraja_-_Gula_Kelapa.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1755 CE – present</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/21st-century">21st century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>sultanate</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Yogyakarta</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Hamengkubuwono I, Hamengkubuwono IX, Hamengkubuwono X</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Sunni Islam</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Javanese</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/indonesia">Indonesia</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Kasultanan Ngayogyakarta Hadiningrat, Yogyakarta</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1890&amp;focus=yogyakarta-sultanate" aria-label="Yogyakarta Sultanate on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7733 2469 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Yogyakarta Sultanate lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M8053 2725L8052 2725M8053 2725L7959 2663M8053 2725L7959 2663M8053 2725L7959 2664M8053 2725L8052 2725"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M8052 2725h0M7959 2663h0M7959 2663h0M7959 2664h0M8052 2725h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M8053 2725h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M8053 2725h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Yogyakarta. <a href="/atlas?year=1890&amp;focus=yogyakarta-sultanate">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
